1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,830
Um, imagine trying to build, like, a massive futuristic highway system,

2
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:08,860
but your only strategy is to just pave over existing dirt paths.

3
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:12,740
Right, without ever actually drawing a blueprint for where the cities are gonna go.

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:22,320
Exactly. And, you know, that is exactly what happens when a digital government tries to upgrade these siloed, duplicated IT systems without a target architecture.

5
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:23,470
Yeah, just doesn't work.

6
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:32,520
So welcome to today's deep dive. Today, we are focusing on phase four, which is plan, design the target architecture of an enterprise architecture.

7
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:39,260
And, um, you can think of an enterprise architecture as basically the master urban plan for a digital government.

8
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:45,600
Right. It is this comprehensive blueprint that makes sure every department isn't just, you know, building their own isolated villages.

9
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:51,150
Yeah, and that blueprint is super crucial because you can't just start paving a road and hope it leads somewhere functional.

10
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:59,480
Exactly. The entire purpose of your architecture work is to produce this target, this defined picture of the future state you are actually building toward.

11
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:05,239
Okay, but my first instinct here would be to just look at our map of this current fragmented state

12
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:10,420
and, I don't know, draw a utopian version of it where everything works perfectly.

13
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,460
Well, that is a very common trap, actually.

14
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:16,840
Oh, really? Yeah, because utopia doesn't fix your specific gaps.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,620
Mm-hmm. You do not invent the target architecture from imagination.

16
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:22,080
Okay, so where does it come from?

17
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:28,160
It is designed by applying strict design rules to the exact gaps you identified in your current state.

18
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:29,220
Ah, got it.

19
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:35,700
The principles you adopt, like the once only rule or reuse before build, they aren't just mission statements.

20
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:39,020
They dictate exactly how the future state is drawn.

21
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:44,390
Since it isn't just based on imagination, let's look at how those design rules, like, physically shape the system.

22
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,220
So they shape the future state across four distinct layers.

23
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:50,560
First, you have target capabilities. Okay.

24
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:55,800
This is where every single capability is owned by exactly one body, which resolves all that duplication.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,539
Makes sense. Second is target data domains, meaning there is one authoritative owner for each specific domain.

26
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,900
Okay, let's ground that in reality for a second.

27
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:14,720
Mm-hmm. Because it sounds great in theory, but, um, how does this fix the reality of citizens having their data rekeyed and captured multiple times?

28
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:22,180
Right. So- Like in the old fragmented system, a teenager getting their driver's license might have to submit their birth certificate

29
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:26,300
to the transport department, the school board, and the tax office separately.

30
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,680
Yeah, and this is where the once only principle does the heavy lifting.

31
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:39,840
Okay. That rule dictates that those three separate duplicated learner lists, they are forced to become a single authoritative learner register.

32
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,740
Ah, so that register becomes the single source of truth.

33
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:47,710
Exactly. Okay, so the data now lives in one place, but what about the systems that actually need to use it?

34
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,360
That brings us to the third layer, which is target shared platforms.

35
00:02:51,390 --> 00:02:58,630
Right. Because of the reuse before build principle, an examination authority doesn't mint its own separate identity records.

36
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:02,000
It simply consumes data from a central identity authority.

37
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,580
So shared platforms operate like, uh, a city's plumbing.

38
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:08,740
Everyone taps into the same pipes instead of digging their own private wells.

39
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:17,720
That is exactly it. And the fourth layer, target technology standards, dictates the building codes for how all those shared pipes and platforms must be constructed.

40
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,160
Just to ensure they fit together. Right.

41
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:23,200
So we've forced all these departments to give up their private learner lists.

42
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:27,220
But if the examination authority doesn't have its own list anymore,

43
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:30,740
how do they actually know who is taking the test?

44
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:33,280
Yeah. I mean, they have to talk to each other now.

45
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:35,860
They do, and that requires an integration map.

46
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:37,540
Okay. The integration map.

47
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:44,100
Yeah. Think of your current state like a house where every single room has its own separate electrical grid.

48
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:48,580
The integration map is the master circuit breaker that finally unifies them.

49
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:55,580
Oh, wow. It details which bodies exchange which data over what mechanism and in what priority order.

50
00:03:55,640 --> 00:04:00,080
So in our example, the examination authority reads the learner data from the learner register.

51
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:04,120
Right. And verifies the identity through the identity authority.

52
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:08,780
Yes, and both of those exchanges happen over a shared data exchange backbone.

53
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:11,730
Which is a building block provided by the digital government authority.

54
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:17,240
Exactly. This entirely replaces the need for paper re-entry.

55
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:20,600
Well, that sounds highly efficient, but I can see a massive pitfall here.

56
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:27,940
Oh. Yeah. How do we guarantee this beautiful integration map doesn't just end up sitting on a desk as an expensive wish list?

57
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:30,820
Well, the architecture must be reachable.

58
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:35,690
Every single element of the target must trace back to a specific gap it closes.

59
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:39,810
Okay. And it has to trace forward to a concrete sourcing decision.

60
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:42,000
Are you gonna build it, buy it, or share it?

61
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:45,940
Meaning a target capability with no realistic way to obtain it

62
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:47,380
is just a wish.

63
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:53,620
Precisely. Because of this, the target architecture and the sourcing decisions must be designed and signed off together.

64
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:57,260
They form the agreed destination that your roadmap will then sequence.

65
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:05,560
To recap, the target architecture is the essential blueprint, bridging the gap between recognizing your digital problems and drawing the roadmap to fix them.

66
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:10,020
Yeah. Built on strict principles, a unified integration map, and concrete sourcing decisions.

67
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,400
But consider this before you go.

68
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:20,450
If every single element in your target architecture must trace directly forward to a sourcing decision, whether that is build, buy, or share,

